## Statement

Let $I_1<I_2<I_3$ be positive reals, let $I:\mathbb R^3\to\mathbb R^3$ be the linear map with $Ie_j=I_je_j$ on the standard orthonormal basis, and consider the torque-free Euler system $l'=F(l)$, $F(l)=l\times(I^{-1}l)$, on $U=\mathbb R^3$ ([[thm-cm-euler-equations-and-torque-free-invariants]], [[def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability]]). Let $L\ne0$. Then $l_0:=Le_2$ is an equilibrium of $F$ and is **not Lyapunov stable**: with $$\varepsilon_0:=\frac{|L|}{2},\qquad a:=\sqrt{\frac{1/I_1-1/I_2}{1/I_2-1/I_3}}>0,\qquad c_0:=a\Bigl(\frac1{I_2}-\frac1{I_3}\Bigr)\frac{\sqrt3}{2}|L|>0,$$ for every $\delta>0$ there is a solution $l$ with $\lVert l(0)-l_0\rVert_2<\delta$ and some $t\ge0$ with $\lVert l(t)-l_0\rVert_2\ge\varepsilon_0$; Moreover, with $\delta_0:=|L|/(2\sqrt{1+a^2})$, the escape can be arranged at a time $t\le c_0^{-1}\log(\delta_0/\eta)$ where $\eta=l_1(0)>0$ may be chosen as small as desired.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** Positive reals $I_1<I_2<I_3$, the diagonal map $I$, the field $F(l)=l\times(I^{-1}l)$ on $U=\mathbb R^3$, and $L\ne0$.

[L1] The system $l'=F(l)$ is read as [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]; $F$ is continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable, since for $\lVert x\rVert_2,\lVert y\rVert_2\le r$ one has $F(x)-F(y)=(x-y)\times(I^{-1}x)+y\times(I^{-1}(x-y))$ and hence $\lVert F(x)-F(y)\rVert_2\le C_r\lVert x-y\rVert_2$ for a finite $C_r$; Picard-Lindelof therefore gives existence and uniqueness, and a solution trapped in a compact subset of $U=\mathbb R^3$ near a finite endpoint extends past it [[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]], [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]], [[thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness]], [[cor-cm-finite-time-breakdown-requires-escape]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]].

[L2] Along every solution the invariants $C(l)=\lVert l\rVert_2^2$ and $E(l)=\tfrac12\langle l,I^{-1}l\rangle$ are constant; in the eigenbasis $Ie_j=I_je_j$ one has $C=\sum_jl_j^2$, $E=\sum_jl_j^2/(2I_j)$, and the component equations $l_1'=(1/I_3-1/I_2)l_2l_3$, $l_2'=(1/I_1-1/I_3)l_1l_3$, $l_3'=(1/I_2-1/I_1)l_1l_2$ [[thm-cm-euler-equations-and-torque-free-invariants]].

[L3] $F(-l)=(-l)\times(I^{-1}(-l))=l\times(I^{-1}l)=-F(l)$ for all $l$, by bilinearity of the cross product and linearity of $I^{-1}$ [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]; hence $t\mapsto-l(t)$ is a solution whenever $t\mapsto l(t)$ is.

[L4] The cross product is bilinear and $\lVert u\times v\rVert_2\le\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2$; the Euclidean inner product is positive definite [[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[thm-cross-product-norm-is-the-two-vector-gram-determinant]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-inner-product-space]].

[L5] If $f:I\to\mathbb R$ is differentiable on an interval with $f'(t)\ge0$ for all interior $t$, then $f$ is nondecreasing; and the product rule $(gh)'=g'h+gh'$ holds; $(e^{\lambda t})'=\lambda e^{\lambda t}$ for real $\lambda$ [[thm-monotonicity-from-the-derivative]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-chain-rule]], [[thm-derivative-of-exponential]].

## Proof

**Proof technique:** direct.

1.1 $F(Le_2)=Le_2\times(L/I_2)e_2=0$ by antisymmetry, so $l_0$ is an equilibrium; and by [L3] it suffices to treat $L>0$: if the conclusion holds for $L_0:=|L|>0$, then for $L<0$ pre- and post-composing the exhibited solutions with $l\mapsto-l$ gives solutions near $Le_2=-|L|e_2$ with the same escape distance. Hence assume $L>0$ in the rest of the proof. [given, L1, L3]

1.2 Let $0<\eta_0<\delta_0:=L/(2\sqrt{1+a^2})$ and let $0<\eta<\eta_0$. Put $s(\eta):=\sqrt{L^2-(1+a^2)\eta^2}>0$ and $l^\eta:=(\eta,\,s(\eta),\,-a\eta)$, so that $C(l^\eta)=L^2$ and $E(l^\eta)=L^2/(2I_2)$: indeed $C(l^\eta)=\eta^2+L^2-(1+a^2)\eta^2+a^2\eta^2=L^2$ and $E(l^\eta)=\tfrac12\bigl(\eta^2/I_1+(L^2-(1+a^2)\eta^2)/I_2+a^2\eta^2/I_3\bigr)=\tfrac12L^2/I_2+\tfrac12\eta^2\bigl(1/I_1-1/I_2-a^2(1/I_2-1/I_3)\bigr)=L^2/(2I_2)$ by the definition of $a$. Also $\lVert l^\eta-l_0\rVert_2\to0$ as $\eta\downarrow0$, since $s(\eta)\to L$. [given, L2, L4, algebra]

2.1 Let $l$ be the solution with $l(0)=l^\eta$; it is defined for all $t\ge0$, because its invariant sphere $C=L^2$ is compact in $U$ and [L1] rules out breakdown inside a compact set. Let $\tau:=\inf\{t>0:l_1(t)\ge\delta_0\text{ or }l_1(t)\le0\}$, so $0<\tau\le+\infty$, and on $(0,\tau)$ one has $0<l_1<\delta_0$. On that interval $l_1(t)^2+l_3(t)^2\le l_1^2+a^2l_1^2<\delta_0^2(1+a^2)=L^2/4$ by [L2] (which gives $l_3=\pm al_1$ on the invariant level set $E=L^2/(2I_2)$, since $E-C/(2I_2)=0$ and $(1/I_2-1/I_3)>0$), so $l_2(t)^2=L^2-l_1^2-l_3^2>3L^2/4$ and $l_2$ has the constant sign $+$ on $[0,\tau)$, its initial sign. Likewise $l_3=\pm al_1$ cannot change sign on $[0,\tau)$ without vanishing, which would force $l_1=l_3=0$ contrary to $l_1>0$; since $l_3(0)=-a\eta<0$, one has $l_3(t)=-al_1(t)$ there. [step 1.2, given, L1, L2, L4, algebra]

3.1 On $(0,\tau)$, the first component equation of [L2] and step 2.1 give $l_1'=(1/I_3-1/I_2)l_2l_3=a(1/I_2-1/I_3)l_2l_1\ \ge c_0l_1$ with $c_0=a(1/I_2-1/I_3)\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}L>0$, because $l_2\ge\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}L$. [step 2.1, L2, algebra]

4.1 By step 3.1 and the product and chain rules [L5], $\bigl(e^{-c_0t}l_1(t)\bigr)'=e^{-c_0t}(l_1'(t)-c_0l_1(t))\ge0$ on $(0,\tau)$; since $e^{-c_0t}l_1(t)$ is continuous on $[0,\tau)$, it is nondecreasing there by [L5], so $l_1(t)\ge\eta e^{c_0t}$ for $t\in[0,\tau)$. If $\tau=+\infty$, this would give $l_1(t)\ge\eta e^{c_0t}>\delta_0$ for $t>c_0^{-1}\log(\delta_0/\eta)$, contradicting $l_1<\delta_0$ on $(0,\tau)$; hence $\tau<+\infty$. [step 2.1, 3.1, given, L5, algebra]

5.1 At the time $\tau$ one has $l_1(\tau)=\delta_0$: indeed $l_1(\tau)\neq0$ by step 4.1 and continuity, so by the definition of $\tau$ and continuity it is the alternative $l_1(\tau)\ge\delta_0$ that occurs, and on $[0,\tau]$ the bound $l_1<\delta_0$ holds on $(0,\tau)$ with $l_1$ continuous, so $l_1(\tau)=\delta_0$; moreover $\tau\le c_0^{-1}\log(\delta_0/\eta)$. Therefore $\lVert l(\tau)-l_0\rVert_2^2\ge l_1(\tau)^2+l_3(\tau)^2=(1+a^2)\delta_0^2=L^2/4$, so $\lVert l(\tau)-l_0\rVert_2\ge L/2=\varepsilon_0$. [step 2.1, 4.1, given, L4, algebra]

6.1 It remains to make the initial point as close to $l_0$ as prescribed: for any $\delta>0$ choose $\eta>0$ with $\eta<\min\{\delta_0,\delta\}$ and $\lVert l^\eta-l_0\rVert_2<\delta$, possible because $\lVert l^\eta-l_0\rVert_2\to0$ by step 1.2. The corresponding solution then has $\lVert l(0)-l_0\rVert_2<\delta$ and, by step 5.1, escapes the ball of radius $\varepsilon_0$ at time $\tau\le c_0^{-1}\log(\delta_0/\eta)$. This is instability of $l_0$ in the sense of [[def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability]], with the explicit time bound of the statement. [step 1.2, 5.1, given, algebra] ∎
